  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\n- Dry forecast into Saturday\n\nA high confidence in dry weather into Saturday continues. Models \nhave been consistent in showing high pressure at both the surface\nand aloft sliding through the Great Lakes region. Precipitable \nwater values do not jump considerably until we get out into the\nSaturday time frame. All ECMWF and GFS ensemble members remain dry\ninto Saturday. Clear to partly cloudy skies are anticipated at\nleast into Friday night.\n\n\n- Chance for rain over the weekend\n\nChances for rain creep into the forecast potentially as early as\nSaturday afternoon, but the best chance for rain will come\nSaturday night and potentially into Sunday. The rain during this\ntime frame will be forced by a strong cold front sliding through\nthe area. The ECWMF is a bit quicker with the front as northern\nand southern stream upper waves are phased. The GFS has more of a\nsplit flow which holds southern stream wave back and is slower in\nits progression. Bottom line, we are looking at some rain over the\nweekend. Precipitation does not look heavy with most areas seeing\nless than a quarter of an inch. At this point, not expecting much\nif any thunder, but it is a strong front and dew points rebound \ninto the 50s for a short time. Thunder is not in the forecast at \nthis time, but we will keep an eye on it.\n\n- Mild temperatures through Saturday then cooling off\n\nNormal highs this time of year are in the 40s across the CWA. We\nwill be in the 50s Wednesday and Thursday and push into the 60s\nfor Friday and Saturday. We will be 20 degrees above normal on the\ndays we push into the 60s. It is that time of year where\ntemperatures rise and fall rapidly with systems moving through.\nColder air will pour in Sunday into Monday with highs dropping\nback into the 40s for Monday and Tuesday.",
